WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.389
"Does Increasing Protein Intake
Slow Age-Related Muscle Mass Loss?"

00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:09.420
Population studies have found
that failing to meet

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.340
the recommended daily intake
of protein

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.860
is correlated
with a lower lean body mass,

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.540
and those exceeding the RDA

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.360
associated with significantly
greater lean body mass

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.120
compared to those failing
to meet the recommended intake.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.000
Greater protein intake
has also been linked

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.160
to greater grip strength
over time,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.700
though not considered enough
to be clinically meaningful.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.220
You don't know
if these correlations

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.320
are even cause-and-effect
though,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.080
until you put it to the test.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.600
To see if increasing
the protein intake

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.160
for the elderly would help,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.740
Harvard researchers
randomized older men

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.300
with moderately impaired
function

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.860
to controlled diets

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.520
offering the recommended
0.8 g/kg of body weight per day

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.480
or bumping them up
to 1.3g/kg a day

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.300
with a whey
and casein protein powder,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.280
with or without
added testosterone.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.080
Six months later,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.200
there was no difference
in lean mass,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.780
muscle strength,
fatigue, walking speed,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:07.740
stair-climbing power,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.040
or any of the other measures
of performance, function,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.900
or well-being
with the added protein,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.480
whether combined
with testosterone or not,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.360
suggesting that the recommended
dietary allowance

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.980
of 0.8 grams of protein

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.480
per healthy kilogram
of body weight,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.400
or about 50 grams a day,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.420
is sufficient to maintain
lean body mass even in old age

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.640
and does not support
this thought

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.200
that adding extra protein

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.140
could promote bulking up
additional lean mass.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.860
Even in studies that do show
an increase in lean mass

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.060
with protein supplementation

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.300
doesn't necessarily mean
an increase in muscle mass.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.040
The field is said to be
"plagued" with researchers

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.160
referring to lean mass
as an indication of muscle mass,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.380
but high protein intake alone

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.800
can cause significant liver
and kidney swelling;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.260
and so, an increase
in total body lean mass

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.280
may just be a reflection of
"increased visceral organ size"

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.140
or water retention,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.540
which also shows up
as lean mass.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.020
For example, a 10-week
randomized controlled trial

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.440
found that elderly men
on twice the protein RDA

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.240
put on three pounds (1.35 kg)
of lean mass

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.340
over that of those
just getting the RDA,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.400
but almost all the mass
was in their trunk.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.920
When cross-sections
of their thigh muscles

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.840
were analyzed using CT scans,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.000
there was
no significant difference

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.560
between the two protein levels.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.860
Similarly, changes in
muscle area measured by CT

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.900
were found to be unrelated
to protein intake

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.640
in a prospective study
that followed

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.160
more than a thousand
older adults over five years.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.820
The gold standard
for assessing muscle size

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.160
is the use of MRI scans.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.400
Randomize hundreds of men
and women over 65 years old

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.940
to a year of a placebo control
containing table sugar,

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.340
versus a low-quality protein
(collagen),

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.660
versus whey protein,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.200
with or without low or high
intensity resistance training,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.420
and the exercise group
gained muscle,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.120
but the protein
didn't help at all.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.100
It made no difference
in muscle size,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.120
strength, or performance.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.040
Even the whey protein,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.180
bumping them from average
protein levels up to 1.5 g/kg,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.560
did no better than feeding them
the equivalent of sugar pills,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.460
and the study was designed
and paid for by a whey company!

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.920
A two-year whey intervention,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.980
one of the largest,
longest such studies ever run,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.460
similarly failed to show
any benefits over placebo.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.620
Put all these studies together

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.080
on using protein
or amino acid supplementation

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.940
to preserve muscle mass
and strength

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.720
in older men and women,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.780
and overall, there was
no significant improvement

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.080
in lean body mass or upper
or lower body muscle strength.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.620
That was without
concurrent exercise, though.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.540
Maybe extra protein works

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.400
when combined
with strength training?

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.060
Very few studies
showed any benefit

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.460
to adding protein
or amino acid supplementation

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.720
during resistance exercise,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.360
but enough trended
in the right direction

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.460
that if you put them
all together,

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:03.660
protein supplementation

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.280
combined with
resistance exercise

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.000
held a small edge
over resistance exercise alone

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.560
among older individuals.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.080
After an average
of 18 weeks of training,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.240
fat-free mass, handgrip,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.520
knee extension,
and leg press strength

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.220
each increased
by an extra half pound (0.23 kg)

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.920
on protein,
though this did not translate

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.320
into any significant improvement
in muscle function

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.060
(such as rising from a chair,

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.680
stair climbing,
or walking speed).

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.420
Now here, "elderly"
was defined as "over 50."

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.160
Restricting the scope

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.040
to studies enrolling
actual elderly individuals

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.780
(average age 70 or older),

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.060
resistance exercise training
worked;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.240
however, they found
no significant additive effects

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.760
to protein supplementation
on any outcome,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.280
lean mass, muscle strength,
or functional abilities.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.860
In non-frail older adults,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.440
extra protein appears
to have little or no effect

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.840
on muscle mass, strength,
or performance

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.380
when taken alone
or added to an exercise regimen.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.140
Okay, but what about
in those who really need it:

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.700
frail individuals?

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.400
One of the first things
doled out by doctors

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.680
is a quote-unquote
"nutrition shake,"

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:11.640
like Ensure®,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.820
which are typically
ultra-processed sugary messes

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.340
of corn syrup, oil,
and protein concentrates,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.080
often laced with
artificial colors,

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.280
flavors, and sweeteners.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.380
Though Big Pharma giants,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.780
like Abbot Laboratories
(makers of Ensure),

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.580
spend millions
of dollars a year

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.920
in lobbying
and campaign contributions

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.740
to help make these products
medicine's go-to choice,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.260
but if you look at the evidence,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.600
a systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.700
of randomized controlled trials
on such drinks

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.140
for the management of frailty

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.640
found no discernable benefit
for any measured outcomes,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.800
muscle mass, muscle strength,
muscle function,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.780
frailty status,
cognitive function,

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:49.740
or mortality.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.220
What about those suffering
from sarcopenia,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.500
accelerated
age-related muscle loss?

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.080
A systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.500
concluded that protein
was a wash across the board

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.020
for improving muscle mass,
strength, or performance

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.840
among elderly
sarcopenic individuals.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.500
The best studies on protein
or amino acid supplements

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.880
for unhealthy older adults

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.380
with acute
or chronic conditions

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.960
also found
no significant benefit.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.400
Researchers have been trying
to find effective ways

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.860
to improve muscle mass
in older people for decades,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:26.460
and so far,
only resistance exercise

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:29.100
has consistently yielded
benefits.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.800
One of the largest
and most rigorous studies

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.020
to treat pre-frail
and frail adults

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.820
was published in 2021.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.820
Hundreds were enrolled to test
the effects of leucine,

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.220
whey protein,
soy protein, creatine,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.200
and a combination
of creatine and whey

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.240
versus a placebo control
(corn starch)

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.300
in the context of a 16-week
resistance training program.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.880
The strength training itself
worked,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.500
increasing muscle mass
and function,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.080
but everything else flopped.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.440
No added benefit to frail
or pre-frail individuals

00:06:58.440 --> 00:06:59.940
taking any of those supplements

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.980
compared to taking
a corn starch placebo.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.640
A similar trial of hundreds
of sarcopenic men and women

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.780
75 and older

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.420
given 40 grams
of whey-enriched protein a day

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.520
combined with exercise
for an entire year

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.680
with a 43-month follow-up

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.600
found that all the extra protein

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.280
did not slow
the deterioration

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.200
of muscle mass
and physical performance

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.300
significantly better
than a placebo,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.040
all the more important

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.420
since it is one of the largest

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:26.820
and longest-lasting
randomized controlled trials

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.380
ever published on the subject.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.520
Consumer Reports
noted that an investigation

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.640
of 134 top-selling
protein powder products

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.040
found virtually
all tested positive

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.600
for at least one heavy metal,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.880
building on their own study
that concluded,

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.920
"You don't need
the extra protein

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.980
or the heavy metals
our tests found."

